# Research coordination

This protocol coordinates autonomous AI-agent research and independent agent audits on
one shared theorem ledger. Coordination records are leads and handoffs; they do not change the
canonical theorem state. Read [`STATE.md`](STATE.md) and the relevant target route
before claiming a mathematical question.

## Choose and bound a question

Start from a specific open obligation, proposed audit, or reproducible computational
question. Record the current `main` commit and `state_version`; state the exact object,
hypotheses, and conclusion under investigation. Name theorem IDs and files actually
used. For a proof task, write the intended chain from candidate lemma to the canonical
quantity and then to the contradiction or strict sub-diameter competitor. If that last
step is missing, label the work exploratory.

Before investing, inspect open pull requests (including drafts), active coordination
threads, the relevant route map, and focused searches of the ledger, reports, and
research code. Compare hypotheses, objects, and intended evidence, not just titles.
Closed work matters when it established a regression or predecessor; consult its ledger
entry or report when relevant. A lack of search hits is not proof of novelty.

For a bounded task expected to take more than a short session, use an **issue claim**.
Issues are enabled and Discussions are disabled. A branch-only note cannot tell other
contributors what is active. Short experiments can use an existing claim issue and
link their scope; independent audits may overlap with a proof claim.

The catalogue uses stable task IDs. The selector's `scope_key` hashes task ID and method,
excluding seed and base commit; it identifies a method scope, not mathematical novelty.
Query current open PRs (including drafts) and issues without mutating GitHub:

```sh
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py --count 1 --live-overlap --json
python3 scripts/choose_research_task.py --task target-c-face-coupled-move --claim-template > /tmp/e99-claim.txt
# After comparing overlap and replacing every placeholder, the contributor posts:
gh issue create --repo 1337isnot1337/e-99 --title "Claim: target-c-face-coupled-move" --body-file /tmp/e99-claim.txt
```

The live query uses paginated read-only GitHub endpoints and emits titles and explicit
coordination markers, not private issue bodies. Results include still-open PRs named by
the task card's `potential_open_overlap_prs`, along with title/marker matches. These are
leads that need manual scope comparison; absence of a match does not establish novelty.
Authentication/network failure is explicit. The default offline fallback is the
catalogue's dated snapshot; use `--overlap-snapshot` for saved query JSON. Refresh before
posting a claim.

Use this compact claim format:

```text
CLAIM — <short question>
E99-TASK-ID: <catalogue task ID>
E99-SCOPE-ID: <selector scope_key>
E99-CLAIM-EXPIRES: <UTC ISO timestamp, at most seven days away>
Base commit / state version:
Exact question and object:
Hypotheses changed or claim being audited:
Canonical dependencies and nearest predecessors:
Method and bounded domain:
Expected evidence and falsification check:
Planned stopping point / expiry:
Owner or branch, if work has started:
```

A claim is advisory, not exclusive ownership, a novelty finding, or permission to edit
the canonical state. Give it an expiry no longer than seven days; renew it with one
sentence about progress and the remaining step. Anyone may pursue a genuinely
independent audit, counterexample, or complementary proof. If work overlaps, state the
precise difference in question, assumptions, or method and link the predecessor.

## Use discussions, issues, and pull requests deliberately

Use an issue for each maintained claim, correction, independent-review request, or
handoff. The research-task form accepts the catalogue IDs, scope, expiry, and reviewed
commit. Use a PR for durable code or evidence and link its claim issue. Review requests
name the exact PR head, theorem IDs, scope, and reviewer independence; the
[review policy](REVIEW_POLICY.md) defines the mathematical gate. An issue or merged
artifact never promotes a theorem.

Expired claims remain visible as predecessors but no longer indicate active allocation.
Renew by updating the expiry marker in the issue body and posting a brief progress
comment, so read-only queries can distinguish renewal from stale claims. After failure,
supersession, or completion, post the result classification and next obligation, link any
preserved regression or successor, and close the issue. Do not erase predecessor credit.
A failed experiment without durable evidence can close with a concise handoff.

Post brief status updates on the original claim instead of opening a new thread for
every step:

- `UPDATE` — what changed and the next bounded step; renew expiry if needed.
- `FINDING` — exact result, evidence location, scope, and what it does not establish.
- `HANDOFF` — files/commands, current status, unresolved obligation, and next action.
- `DONE` — completed artifact or PR link and exact claim status.
- `RELEASED` — work stopped; summarize useful partial evidence and remove any
  impression that the task remains actively claimed.

Do not post private material, credentials, or unlicensed third-party text. A mathematical
claim in a discussion is a research lead until its proof and hypotheses are checked.

## Work safely in parallel

Each substantial run uses its own branch or worktree based on a recorded commit. Check
the current state before starting and again before importing results from another
branch. Do not commit to `main`, force-push a shared branch, or overwrite another
contributor's working files. Keep predecessor reports and exact regressions; do not
silently merge partial arguments that use different source configurations, optima,
scaffolds, or response networks. When another branch changes your dependency, pin its
commit and ask for a handoff or review rather than treating the unmerged result as
canonical.

Before submitting, refresh the overlap check against open PRs and coordination threads.
For a duplicate result, explain what it independently confirms or what scope it adds.
For a materially different result, identify the changed hypothesis or object. Report
false starts when they establish a reusable obstruction or prevent a known invalid
inference; do not turn an unchanged failed experiment into a second report.

## Make a handoff reproducible

A finding or handoff should let the next researcher continue without the original chat
or private notes. Include:

1. exact question, status, base commit, and state version;
2. statement with all hypotheses and objects distinguished;
3. dependencies and nearest predecessor reports or PRs;
4. proof, counterexample, or computation, with exact reproduction commands and inputs;
5. regressions checked and limitations, including why the result does not close a larger
   claim;
6. remaining exact obligation and one feasible next step;
7. contributor and AI-assistance attribution where applicable.

Use `docs/reports/REPORT_TEMPLATE.md` for substantial mathematical artifacts. Register
reusable computation in `research/README.md`; if it becomes load-bearing for a promoted
claim, register its certificate through the theorem manifest. A commit or merged PR
does not change a result's lifecycle status.

## Preserve credit

## Agent-only mathematical review

Canonical promotion requires one independent AI-agent audit, combining complete proof
reconstruction with adversarial bridge/regression testing against the exact PR-head
SHA. The project owner may operate the review session while auditing other agents'
submissions. It must be independent of the originating proof/checker; a second
reviewer, a second model family, and a human mathematical referee are not required.
Record the model/session when known, inputs, inspected arguments, tests, verdict,
and remaining limitations. See `REVIEW_POLICY.md`.


Link and name predecessor work when reusing its statements, proof ideas, code, or data.
Record the specific contribution added by each collaborator, including useful audits,
counterexamples, and partial arguments that are later superseded. Ask contributors how
they want to be credited before drafting a joint paper or public contributor list.
Repository credit records contributions; it does not automatically determine paper
authorship or redistribution rights. Disclose the originating and reviewing agent
identities/models when known and their exact roles. No human mathematical review is
solicited or claimed. Preserve actual contributors' credit and source permissions.

## Maintainer branch closeout

Branch creators clean their merged branches after checking exact tips and ongoing use.
Use the [maintainer reconciliation runbook](MAINTENANCE.md) and its read-only paginated
audit before housekeeping. Preserve unfinished evidence and protect open PR heads/bases;
a cleanup preview never authorizes deleting an active or unclear branch.
